Refinement to D4 (please fold into your answer; it is the sharpest version of the question). The sheet structure is finite and known (6 rows × 26 cols by default, stored row_count/col_count), and "bounds" can mean two different things:

(a) negative index (col A → left, row 1 → up) — reachable only through a copy offset;
(b) an index beyond the declared structure — reachable through a copy offset that moves a reference past the last row/column (e.g. a formula referencing A6 = row index 5 in a 6-row sheet, copied one row down → A7 = row index 6, which does not exist), and also by simply typing a reference like =A7 / =A1000 / =ZZ1.

My revised proposal:
- `rewriteRefsOnCopy(formula, dRow, dCol, bounds)` receives the target worksheet bounds and rewrites any reference token (single cell OR contiguous range) that leaves those bounds into the literal `#REF!` token, so a copy past the last row yields `=#REF!` in the formula bar and `#REF!` in the grid (satisfying the REQ-4-1-2 sentence in both the negative-offset and past-the-last-row direction);
- evaluation-time: a single-cell reference outside the structure → `#REF!` (so a typed `=A7` is "an invalid reference displays #REF!" per REQ-4-2-2) BUT a contiguous range is clipped to the structure, with cells outside simply not existing (skipped like blanks), so a typed `=SUM(B1:B1000)` still returns the sum of the cells that exist;
- this asymmetry (single ref = demand for one specific cell that cannot exist → #REF!; range = set of existing cells, clipped) is chosen so a whole-column aggregate does not explode.

Questions: (1) is that asymmetry defensible, or does it create a self-contradiction an external harness could trip on (e.g. rejecting a clipped range, or expecting =A7 to be blank/0)? (2) Is there a cheaper single rule that satisfies both requirement sentences? (3) Does the requirement sentence "If the offset moves a relative reference outside the worksheet bounds, the target formula bar displays =#REF!" argue that the rewrite MUST be bounds-aware (which means the promised signature `rewriteRefsOnCopy(formula, dRow, dCol)` needs an extra bounds argument — I can still change it since it is not yet published)?

